now the wearing of masks is set to be a  personal choice  from july the 19th but some pubs  restaurants and shops say they will  still encourage customers to wear them  meanwhile the mayor of greater  manchester andy burnham has said it is  reckless to lift this requirement to  wear masks on public  transport well i'm pleased to say that  andy burnham joins us now  from lee mr burnham good afternoon to  you  good afternoon and boris johnson in  prime minister's questions earlier on  today  refuted the accusation that his plans  were reckless and he gave his reasons  behind that  what did you make of it because you have  yourself called his plans reckless  well i should start by saying i broadly  support the road map and the  approach the government is taking but i  do think they've gone a step too  far with the removal of the requirement  to wear masks particularly on public  transport  there is a question there about whether  they should be required an essential  retail  as well and that's because this isn't a  matter of individual  freedom as as it's been said around  freedom day  one person's freedom is another person's  loss of freedom because if  you're somebody who um has um  a compromised immune system or somebody  who's traveling by  public transport for their chemotherapy  you know they will be put in a very  different difficult position  and i don't think it's too much to ask  at this stage knowing that people  don't like wearing masks but  nevertheless this is about still looking  after everybody as we go through  the later stages of this of this  pandemic and i think the government went  too far  in removing the requirement to wear  masks and i know that  your freedom day for some will be loss  of freedom date for others  so what would you like to see stay you  mentioned the wearing of masks on public  transport  and in essential retail for example so  i'm presuming you mean things like  chemists and supermarkets is there  anything else you would like to see  stay for the foreseeable future  well that is the main thing and i think  the difference is  places that people go by choice so if  people are going to go to bars  restaurants or even nightclubs  come the 19th of july that is a choice  and you know where you're  going into and you're aware of the risks  there are many people in greater  manchester who have no choice  but to use public transport or to go  into the  shops that you you mentioned essential  retail  and i think that's a very different  thing when people are in that  environment  and i think it's unfair that some people  may feel now very uncomfortable in that  environment or worse not even able to to  go in there  and and use those facilities so i think  that is where the government has got  things  wrong more broadly as i say i  can see a lot of logic in what they are  doing and of course we do have to  to learn to live with the virus and and  return as much as we can to normal life  the risk though of course is  if you remove all the restrictions and  the requirements  it kind of suggests or it sends a  message that we're back to normal that  we're out of the pandemic and of course  that isn't the case and i i do have a  concern  that later in the year the government  will have to backtrack in some way if  if um if we see and rising cases or a  new variant or  or some other change in the way things  are are  evolving mr berlin perhaps you can  remind us what the situation is in  greater manchester at the moment i  understand infection numbers are going  up but  at a slower rate than the national  average  yes i mean infection rates are going up  in  in each of our 10 boroughs um and the  the rate of increase has slowed as you  as you say but  they're still high there's no getting  away from that  and that is why you know we are in a  different position  and it's why i do have some concerns  about what's been been announced but as  elsewhere  the link between hospitalization is  is um broken to a some considerable  degree not  completely because there are still  people in hospital and those numbers  have have increased but it's not  the same as we saw uh in 2020 or  or indeed early 2021 so you know it's it  it's a difficult thing i think when you  have the government saying we could be  up to a hundred thousand cases a day in  a  few weeks time as the health secretary  said this week oh and by the way we're  removing all restrictions  i think that the kind of contradiction  there has caused some uncertainty  and some unease amongst our residents  and i do think when the prime minister  goes to the liaison committee today i do  think he needs to be asked  about this uh and to to give the  evidence as to why this is a  uh a step that we're able to take at  this stage yeah  nice of you to mention that that promo  is our 3 30 taking of that live here on  sky news you can watch it plenty of  questions for the prime minister to  answer  i want to talk about education because  obviously there's the july 19th date but  there's also been this date of august  the 16th put in people's diary  self-isolation is going to continue up  until that date and that has a big  impact  on young people and schools  what are your thoughts on the  self-isolation restrictions and when  they're being lifted and what are you  thinking about school bubbles coming to  an end do you support the measures that  are going to have an effect on education  i certainly know that there's been a bit  of an intake of breath around the organ  states  for the end of self-isolation and you  know that definitely feels too  too soon here uh given that  as i've just said the health secretary  is predicting cases at 100 000  um a day later later this month or into  next month so that seems again an  odd decision i would be saying to the  government they should be going the  opposite  way giving more support for people to  self-isolate who need  to do so there is a different situation  in schools where  much of the um of the cases is  related to people with no illness so  that people  who are asymptomatic and the situation  in schools have become unsustainable  so i do think the education secretary  was right to  announce the changes that he that he has  um  i mean it's a period of uncharted  territory that we're going into no one  quite knows now  how things are going to unfold over over  the coming  weeks and i think we still have to er on  the side of  of caution and hence my kind of concern  at some of what's what's been announced  uh but i do think the position in  schools have definitely become  unsustainable  and i know it's been broadly welcomed  the changes that the health sec the  education secretary has announced  how frustrating is it for you to be  mayor of greater manchester at the  moment because  obviously we're talking about powers  going to regions so you can make your  own decisions but  in an instance like this the prime  minister is almost saying to you  you know make up your own mind make up  your own decisions but you have  no authority if you see what i mean in  terms of  if you want to enforce mass on public  transport in greater manchester  or in shops you can't really you can  only advise people so what are you  saying to the people of greater  manchester today  ahead of these two key dates it it is  difficult  uh sergey and i i do think you're right  to point to that because  when a statement is made by the  government it's hard for us to take a  different position  um particularly given here we only  control the tram system  so we can only if we wanted to we can  only enforce masks there we can't  enforce them on buses  or trains because of the way transport  is organized outside of london i think  there's more ability to enforce in  london  than we have so that makes it very hard  because obviously it's a recipe for  confusion or tension  if we try to do that and it is a bit  frustrating again that there was no  genuine consultation with 